      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim was to determine whether rapid ventricular pacing can protect against the ventricular arrhythmias occurring during a subsequent coronary artery occlusion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "The effect was examined of two 2 min periods of pacing (300 beats.min-1) in chloralose-urethane anaesthetised dogs on a subsequent 25 min coronary artery occlusion. Ventricular arrhythmias, ST segment elevation, and inhomogeneity of conduction were analysed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "EXPERIMENTAL MATERIAL",
          "content": "25 anaesthetised mongrel dogs in a restricted body weight range were used."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Preocclusion pacing reduced the severity of occlusion induced ST segment elevation, degree of inhomogeneity, and arrhythmias: ventricular premature beats were reduced from 528(SEM 40) to 136(45), and there were lower incidences of ventricular fibrillation (0% v 47%) and ventricular tachycardia (30% v 80%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Rapid ventricular pacing \"preconditions\" the myocardium in a manner similar to that following short coronary artery occlusions. Short periods of ischaemia no matter how induced protect the heart against the arrhythmogenic effect of a prolonged coronary artery occlusion."
        }
      ]},
